{"v":"3.0.0","project":{"accession":"PRJNA1544744","title":"Food waste fermentation Metagenome","description":"The anaerobic fermentation for volatile fatty acids (VFAs) production from food waste is a promising valorization pathway. However, conventional acidogenic fermentation inevitably releases substantial CO2, leading to both organic carbon loss and greenhouse gas effects. Here, we propose re-directing the fermentation pathway towards acetogenesis as a strategy to enhance VFAs yield. By introducing a synthetic acetogenic consortium with bicarbonate as a soluble CO2 source, net VFAs production reached 39.0 g COD/L, representing a 51.2% increase over the blank control, while bicarbonate supplementation contributed an additional 9.1% increase relative to the inoculated system with pH control alone. Metatranscriptomic profiling showed higher expression of genes encoding the WLP (log2FC = 3.76-8.47) and energy-conserving complexes (ferredoxin-NAD+ oxidoreductase, hydrogenase, and electron transfer flavoprotein; log2FC=4.37-9.59) in the consortium-bicarbonate groups relative to the blank control. Stable carbon isotope patterns of acetate (delta13C) were consistent with a contribution of WLP-associated CO2 conversion into VFAs. The resulting VFAs-rich effluent as an denitrification carbon source achieved total nitrogen concentrations below 10 mg N/L under the tested conditions. This study demonstrates that promoting acetogenesis over conventional acidogenesis can enhance VFA production and improve carbon conversion, offering a potential strategy for carbon-efficient organic waste valorization.","organism":"food fermentation metagenome","data_type":"Metagenome","center_name":"Wuxi University","submission_date":"2026/10/16 00:00","last_update":null},"sample_cols":["accession","title","collection_date","country","region","city","lat","lon","urban_status","urban_context","eligibility_reason","isolation_source","env_broad_scale","env_local_scale","env_medium","host","locat